{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Eightfold.NonetMixingUnconstrained","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Eightfold.NonetMixingUnconstrained","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":6,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Eightfold.NonetMixingUnconstrained.targets_can_be_distinct","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Eightfold.NonetMixingUnconstrained.targets_can_be_distinct","kind":"theorem","line":145,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Eightfold/NonetMixingUnconstrained.lean#L145-L148","doc":"And the construction is not degenerate in the other direction either: the\ntwo target masses can be distinct, so this is a statement about a genuine\ntwo-state mixing problem rather than a repeated eigenvalue. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Eightfold.NonetMixingUnconstrained.mixing_not_forced_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Eightfold.NonetMixingUnconstrained.mixing_not_forced_zero","kind":"theorem","line":137,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Eightfold/NonetMixingUnconstrained.lean#L137-L143","doc":"The construction genuinely requires **nonzero** mixing whenever the octet\nmember lies strictly between the two physical masses — which is the observed\nsituation in both nonets.  Witnessed over `ℤ` at `d₁ = 1`, `l₁ = 0`, `l₂ = 2`:\n`esq = (1 − 0)(2 − 1) = 1 ≠ 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Eightfold.NonetMixingUnconstrained.charPoly_zero_mixing_eq_linear_factors","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Eightfold.NonetMixingUnconstrained.charPoly_zero_mixing_eq_linear_factors","kind":"theorem","line":121,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Eightfold/NonetMixingUnconstrained.lean#L121-L130","doc":"**Zero mixing is exactly the unmixed case.**  When `e = 0` the\ncharacteristic polynomial factors as `(x − d₁)(x − d₂)`, i.e. the physical\nmasses *are* the octet and singlet entries and no mixing occurs.\n\nThe mixing is therefore carried entirely by `e`: it is the single parameter\nthe symmetry leaves free, and the one whose observed value the vector and\npseudoscalar nonets disagree about. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Eightfold.NonetMixingUnconstrained.mixing_unconstrained","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Eightfold.NonetMixingUnconstrained.mixing_unconstrained","kind":"theorem","line":103,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Eightfold/NonetMixingUnconstrained.lean#L103-L119","doc":"**The nonet mixing is unconstrained by the octet-breaking ansatz.**\n\nGiven the octet member `d₁` that GMO fixes, *every* pair of physical masses\n`(l₁, l₂)` is realised by some singlet entry `d₂` and some squared mixing\n`esq`.  The ansatz therefore says **nothing** about where the physical states\nsit or how strongly they mix.\n\nContrast `GellMannOkuboIdentity.gmo_cannot_discriminate` and\n`DecupletEqualSpacing.equal_spacing_cannot_discriminate`, where the ansatz\nforces the answer and the relation is consequently content-free.  Here it\nforces nothing, so the measured mixing **is** information — and deriving it\nwould be a real prediction. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:rem:vector-meson-nonet","label":"rem:vector-meson-nonet","md":"quantum-observable-universe/eightfold-way/vector-meson-nonet.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Eightfold.NonetMixingUnconstrained.charPoly_factors_of_trace_det_match","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Eightfold.NonetMixingUnconstrained.charPoly_factors_of_trace_det_match","kind":"theorem","line":90,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Eightfold/NonetMixingUnconstrained.lean#L90-L101","doc":"**The key identity.**  With the octet entry `d₁` held fixed — it is what\nGell-Mann–Okubo determines — the singlet entry `d₂` and the mixing `e` can be\nchosen so that the characteristic polynomial is exactly `(x − l₁)(x − l₂)`, for\n**any** targets `l₁, l₂`.\n\nBoth choices are forced and explicit:\n`d₂ = l₁ + l₂ − d₁` (matching the trace) and `e² = (d₁ − l₁)(l₂ − d₁)`\n(matching the determinant). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Eightfold.NonetMixingUnconstrained.charPoly","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Eightfold.NonetMixingUnconstrained.charPoly","kind":"def","line":79,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Eightfold/NonetMixingUnconstrained.lean#L79-L88","doc":"The characteristic polynomial of the symmetric `2 × 2` nonet mass matrix\n\n        ⎡ d₁  e ⎤\n        ⎣ e   d₂ ⎦\n\nwritten out as `x² − (trace)·x + (determinant)`.  Stated as a plain polynomial\nexpression rather than via `Matrix.charpoly` so that the statement stays\nelementary and division-free. ","signatureChunk":0}]}